id: rule-array-assign-correct
title: The correct rule
topic: Arrays
example-of: ARRAY-ASSIGN
derived: true

task:
  Use the rule ARRAY-ASSIGN to decide whether this triple can be derived.

given:
  Position written: $i$
  Value written: $1$

pre: j != i && a[j] = 0 && 0 <= i < sizeof(a)
post: a[j] = 0

program:
  {P}
  {R1}
  a[i] := 1      // w
  {Q}

verdict: valid

step: The substitution of the array
rule: ARRAY-ASSIGN
focus: w
text:
  The rule replaces the array $a$ in $Q$ by the updated array
  $a[i |-> 1]$, which is $a$ with the value 1 at position $i$.
calc:
  (a[j] = 0)[a <- a[i |-> 1]]     // Q is a[j] = 0
  = a[i |-> 1][j] = 0             // a replaced by a[i |-> 1]
set: R1 = a[i |-> 1][j] = 0

step: The two cases of the read
rule: ARRAY-ASSIGN
focus: R1
text:
  We do not know whether $j$ is equal to $i$. At position $i$ the updated
  array holds 1; at any other position $j$ it holds $a[j]$.
calc:
  a[i |-> 1][j] = 0
  = (j = i && 1 = 0) || (j != i && a[j] = 0)    // read of a[i |-> 1] at position j
  = j != i && a[j] = 0                          // 1 = 0 is false, and false || A is A
set: R1 = j != i && a[j] = 0

step: The write must be defined
rule: ARRAY-ASSIGN
focus: w
text:
  The write is at position $i$, so the rule also adds $def(a[i])$, which
  is $0 <= i < sizeof(a)$.
set: R1 = j != i && a[j] = 0 && 0 <= i < sizeof(a)

step: P implies R1
rule: CONSEQUENCE
focus: P, R1
text:
  >>> «P» ==> «R1»
vc: j != i && a[j] = 0 && 0 <= i < sizeof(a) ==> j != i && a[j] = 0 && 0 <= i < sizeof(a)
why:
  The two formulas are the same, so the rule derives the triple.
